  <RESPONSE>
    Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) is a network protocol used to prevent loops in Ethernet networks. It works by identifying redundant paths in a network topology and logically disabling specific ports to ensure there is only one active path between any two nodes, creating a loop-free "tree" structure.
    
    If a primary link fails, STP automatically detects the failure and enables a previously blocked redundant path to restore connectivity.
